---
// The front page: one column of prose, code and figures. Every listing and every number
// below is read at build time from the compiler vendored at vendor/shader-dsl.
import { Code } from 'astro-expressive-code/components'
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import CodeFrame from '../components/CodeFrame.astro'
import DiagnosticBlock from '../components/DiagnosticBlock.astro'
import InstallCommand from '../components/InstallCommand.astro'
import LayoutTable from '../components/LayoutTable.astro'
import ShaderCanvas from '../components/ShaderCanvas.astro'
import type { CanvasText } from '../components/ShaderCanvas.astro'
import { exampleFile, facts, hero } from '../lib/examples.ts'
import { links } from '../lib/links.ts'
import { typedError } from '../lib/typed-error.ts'

const glslTarget = facts.glslTarget
const std = facts.layoutStandards[0]
const err = typedError()
const origin = facts.deepZoomOrigin
const split = facts.splitLabels
if (!split) throw new Error("[index] 'fp64-deep-zoom' declares no split labels")

const metaballs: CanvasText = {
  neutral: `Metaballs, from examples/${exampleFile('metaballs')}. The still is captured at build time.`,
  webgpu: 'Metaballs. The WGSL emit is drawing this frame on WebGPU.',
  webgl2: `Metaballs. The ${glslTarget} emit is drawing this frame on WebGL2.`,
  none: 'Metaballs. Rendered at build time; this browser has no WebGPU or WebGL2.',
  reduced: `Metaballs, compiled to WGSL and to ${glslTarget}. One frame is drawn here, since this system asks for reduced motion.`,
}

// The pair below is captioned from the backend that drew each frame, so a reader is never
// told they are looking at WebGPU when WebGL2 drew it. Every state gets a string that is
// true of it, including the states a prop makes unreachable today.
const gpuFrame: CanvasText = {
  neutral: 'The WGSL emit, drawn on WebGPU at build time.',
  webgpu: 'The WGSL emit, drawn here on WebGPU.',
  webgl2: `WebGPU is not available here, so the ${glslTarget} emit drew this frame on WebGL2.`,
  none: 'The WGSL emit, drawn on WebGPU at build time. This browser has no WebGPU or WebGL2.',
}
const glFrame: CanvasText = {
  neutral: `The ${glslTarget} emit, drawn on WebGL2 at build time.`,
  webgpu: 'The WGSL emit, drawn here on WebGPU.',
  webgl2: `The ${glslTarget} emit, drawn here on WebGL2.`,
  none: `The ${glslTarget} emit, drawn on WebGL2 at build time. This browser has no WebGPU or WebGL2.`,
}

const deepZoom: CanvasText = (() => {
  const text =
    `One world coordinate drawn twice: ${split[0]} on the left, ${split[1]} on the right. ` +
    `The ${split[0]} half draws the stripes as steps because one ${split[0]} ulp at this distance is ` +
    'coarser than the fraction a stripe needs; the other half draws them smooth.'
  return {
    neutral: text,
    webgpu: text,
    webgl2: text,
    none: `${text} Rendered at build time; this browser has no WebGPU or WebGL2.`,
  }
})()

const printCommands = ['npx tsx examples/print.ts', 'npx tsx examples/print.ts hillshade'].join('\n')
---

<Base>
  <article class="wrap doc">
    <h1>TypeShade</h1>
    <p>
      TypeShade is a TypeScript library for writing shaders. One typed module emits WGSL for WebGPU
      and {glslTarget} for WebGL2. The same module also compiles to a CPU function in double precision;
      the test suite uses it to check the compiler's algebra. Version {facts.nextVersion} is not on npm
      yet; the repository is the package, used as a git submodule.
    </p>

    <ShaderCanvas example="metaballs" aspect="2/1" eager caption={metaballs} />

    <section>
      <h2>Quick start</h2>
      <InstallCommand />
      <p>
        The package ships TypeScript source, so the consuming build needs a toolchain that compiles
        TypeScript. <code>tsc -p {facts.mirrorPath}</code> type-checks the submodule by itself; it needs no
        install and no <code>node_modules</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The listing below is the fragment stage of the gradient example, {hero.authoredLines} lines as
        authored in <code>{hero.file}</code>. Parameters arrive as typed nodes, uniform fields are read
        through <code>U</code>, the block declared further up the same file, and the returned value fixes
        the return type.
      </p>
      <CodeFrame label={`Authored fragment, ${hero.file}`}>
        <Code code={hero.authored} lang="ts" title={hero.file} />
      </CodeFrame>
      <p>That emits a WGSL entry point:</p>
      <CodeFrame label="The emitted WGSL fragment entry point">
        <Code code={hero.wgsl} lang="wgsl" />
      </CodeFrame>
      <p>and a {glslTarget} <code>main</code> for the same stage:</p>
      <CodeFrame label={`The emitted ${glslTarget} fragment main`}>
        <Code code={hero.glsl} lang="glsl" />
      </CodeFrame>
      <p>
        The whole module is {hero.emit.wgslLines} lines of WGSL; the GLSL vertex stage is{' '}
        {hero.emit.glslVertexLines} lines and the fragment stage {hero.emit.glslFragmentLines}. Uniform
        types, byte offsets, bind-group entries and entry signatures come from{' '}
        <a href={links.reflectApi.href}>reflect()</a>, which reads the same intermediate representation
        and stays off the emit path, so a host can pack its uniform buffer from that layout.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why this exists</h2>
      <p>
        A shader that has to run on WebGL2 and on WebGPU exists twice. The two languages disagree about
        types, entry points, resource binding and precision, so the second copy is a rewrite. A fix that
        lands in one copy and misses the other only shows up on the machines that take the other path. A
        reviewer has to read two dialects and hold both in their head to decide whether they still mean
        the same thing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The web is in the middle of one such move. The{' '}
        <a href={links.maplibreRoadmap.href}>{links.maplibreRoadmap.label}</a>, the{' '}
        <a href={links.deckglWebgpu.href}>{links.deckglWebgpu.label}</a> and the{' '}
        <a href={links.pixijsMigration.href}>{links.pixijsMigration.label}</a> each describe a WebGPU path
        arriving beside an existing WebGL one, so a custom layer written as GLSL will need a WGSL copy. In
        the <a href={links.survey.href}>{facts.survey.title}</a>, {facts.survey.figure64}% of the{' '}
        {facts.survey.n}+ shader developers surveyed adapt shaders across platforms, APIs or tools, and
        nearly {facts.survey.figure10}% describe it as a significant engineering cost or one of their
        largest.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What it does</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Typed functions, entry points, control flow and declarators for uniform blocks, storage buffers,
          IO structs, textures and samplers.
        </li>
        <li>A wrong-typed return, or a field the uniform struct does not have, is a TypeScript error.</li>
        <li>
          Optimizer passes over the IR: common subexpression and dead code elimination, loop-invariant
          hoisting, constant folding and algebraic rewrites.
        </li>
        <li>
          Coded diagnostics with a one-line hint, aggregated by <code>validate()</code> and rendered by{' '}
          <code>diagnose()</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Reflection over the IR: bind groups, {facts.layoutStandards.join(' and ')} struct layouts and
          entry signatures.
        </li>
        <li>A WGSL backend for WebGPU, byte-stable across runs.</li>
        <li>
          A {glslTarget} backend for vertex and fragment stages, checked on a WebGL2 context.
        </li>
        <li>Emulated double precision, authored with the same syntax as f32.</li>
        <li>A gather-only compute tier that lowers to WebGPU and to a WebGL2 emulation.</li>
        <li>A CPU oracle: the same module compiled to an f64 JavaScript function.</li>
        <li>
          <code>variantFamily</code>: one module emitted N ways against a shared prelude, validated as a
          unit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>semanticDiff</code>: two modules compared across interface, resources, constants and the
          control-flow skeleton.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        TypeShade has no renderer. It returns strings and reflection metadata; creating pipelines, binding
        resources and issuing draws stay with the host. SPIR-V, MSL and HLSL come out of naga or Tint, fed
        the WGSL, because every native host already reaches WGSL through Dawn or wgpu.{' '}
        <code>semanticDiff</code> is a review aid for an emit change; it does not compare expression trees,
        so it cannot show two modules are equivalent.
      </p>

      <h3>A misspelt field is a compile error</h3>
      <p>
        The uniform block is declared once, and every field read is typed against that declaration.
        Misspell one and TypeScript says so in the editor, before a string reaches a GPU.
      </p>
      <figure class="figure">
        <DiagnosticBlock />
        <LayoutTable />
        <figcaption class="figure-caption">
          The wrong read on line {err.wrongLine}, the diagnostic it produces, and the {std} layout{' '}
          <a href={links.reflectApi.href}>reflect()</a> recovers for the block it reads from: {err.layout.size}{' '}
          bytes over {err.layout.fields.length} fields. The gap after the first field is alignment.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3>Emulated double precision</h3>
      <p>
        Neither WGSL nor {glslTarget} has a double-precision float. TypeShade emulates one as an unevaluated
        pair of f32 values, and lowers arithmetic, comparisons and a set of builtins into calls on that
        pair. An operation outside that set fails at emit with a coded error. What you write is unchanged:
        only the declared type differs. In the deep-zoom example the <code>{origin.name}</code> field is
        declared <code>{origin.sourceType}</code> in the source and occupies a{' '}
        <code>{origin.layoutType}</code> slot in the uniform block, which the host fills with the split
        pair.
      </p>
      <p>
        A module that does f64 arithmetic also carries an injected guard texture binding for the host to
        fill with a single white texel. A downstream shader compiler cannot fold a texel read, and without
        one it is free to optimise the error-compensation terms away. Each operation costs several times
        its f32 equivalent, so the type is opted into per value. The example is{' '}
        <a href={links.deepZoom.href}>{links.deepZoom.label}</a>.
      </p>
      <ShaderCanvas example="fp64-deep-zoom" aspect="3/1" still split caption={deepZoom} />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How it is checked</h2>
      <p>
        The repository's CI runs these on every push and pull request, in{' '}
        <a href={links.ciGates.href}>{links.ciGates.label}</a>:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          The same module compiles to a CPU function that runs in f64 arithmetic. In its default mode only
          its equality tests round to f32 first, so they agree with the GPU; an f32 mode that rounds after
          every operation is opt-in. The test suite checks that function against known
          answers and against a second CPU backend, generated JavaScript, which must match it bit for bit.
          It says nothing about what rounding does on a driver, and no GPU output is compared against it in
          this repository. <a href={links.oracle.href}>{links.oracle.label}</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          The compile gate emits every registered example, hands each WGSL emit to Tint inside headless
          Chromium, and compiles and links both {glslTarget} stages of every renderable example on a real
          WebGL2 context. It also hands each compiler a shader that cannot compile: if either accepts that
          non-program, the gate fails and the verdicts on the examples do not count.{' '}
          <a href={links.compileGate.href}>{links.compileGate.label}</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          Golden files hold the emitted bytes of every example, so any change in a backend surfaces as a
          diff in review. <a href={links.goldens.href}>{links.goldens.label}</a>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The gradient pass from Quick start, drawn once by each backend. No pixel comparison between the two
        is committed yet.
      </p>
      <div class="figure-pair">
        <ShaderCanvas example="gradient" aspect="3/2" still caption={gpuFrame} />
        <ShaderCanvas example="gradient" stillId="gradient-webgl2" aspect="3/2" still forceWebGl2 caption={glFrame} />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Examples</h2>
      <p>
        There are {facts.examples} runnable examples in the repository, covering cartographic passes, the
        ShaderToy-era screen-space effects, the emulated-double tier and a compute kernel.{' '}
        {facts.bothTargets} of them emit WGSL and {glslTarget} from one source. The compute kernel has no
        vertex or fragment stage to emit as {glslTarget}, so it emits WGSL and reflection; its WebGL2 path
        is the opt-in emulation. {facts.fp64Examples} use emulated double precision. The renderable ones
        are exported from <a href={links.examplesIndex.href}>{links.examplesIndex.label}</a>; browse{' '}
        <a href={links.examplesDir.href}>{links.examplesDir.label}</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first command below prints WGSL, GLSL and reflection for every example; the second does one by
        id.
      </p>
      <CodeFrame label="Printing an example's emits">
        <Code code={printCommands} lang="bash" />
      </CodeFrame>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Status</h2>
      <p>
        Pre-release. The GitHub repository is a mirror of the upstream tree and sits at version{' '}
        {facts.mirrorVersion}; {facts.nextVersion} is the release the npm name{' '}
        <a href={links.npm.href}>typeshade</a> is reserved for. It is {facts.license} licensed, has{' '}
        {facts.runtimeDeps} runtime dependencies (<a href={links.packageJson.href}
          >{links.packageJson.label}</a
        >) and carries {facts.testFiles} test files. The manifest and the imports still use the old scoped
        name and are renamed at that tag, with the old name kept as an alias for one major version.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href={links.docs.href}>README</a> and the <a href={links.guide.href}>authoring guide</a> go
        further, including the shapes each backend refuses and the subset each tier accepts. Issues are
        welcome on the mirror; pull requests cannot be merged there yet, because changes still land
        upstream and this tree is fast-forwarded from there.{' '}
        <a href={links.releases.href}>Watch releases</a> if you want to hear about {facts.nextVersion}.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
